<!-- cell: title-page id="78f7065b-3775-49a8-8ca4-158112262dd3" title="The Best Man's Cards" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="e6805c73-2a18-4366-90a1-cd47838aedab" -->

Gordon Pike had rehearsed his best man's speech forty-one times, once for every year he had known Dev. He had rehearsed it to the bathroom mirror, to the cat and, on one low evening, to a man at a bus stop who had not asked to hear it, because the thought of standing in front of a crowd with nothing to say kept him awake at night.

The speech lived on six laminated cue cards in the inside pocket of his hired jacket. At four o'clock the rain began drumming on the marquee at Hollinsworth Farm, where a hundred and twenty guests sat elbow to elbow under the dripping canvas, and Gordon patted his pocket and found it empty.

He searched his other pockets, his shoes and the cake table, where a steady drip from the roof was hollowing a crater in the top tier. The cards were gone.

"And now," said the master of ceremonies, "the best man."

Gordon stood. A hundred and twenty faces turned towards him, and the drip moved to the back of his neck. His mouth opened, and nothing came out of it but a small, high noise, like a kettle reconsidering.

In the front row sat Aunt Maud, the groom's great-aunt, who was ninety-two, heard roughly one word in three, and had never once in her life doubted herself. She gave him a thumbs-up with tremendous confidence.

"Dev," Gordon began, because his friend's name was the only word he could find. "Dev is my oldest friend. When we were nineteen he lent me his last tenner so I could take a girl to the pictures. She left halfway through, and he never asked for the tenner back." The guests laughed, and Gordon felt the floor return beneath his feet.

Then, carried away, he told the one story he had sworn to Dev he would take to his grave. "And he failed his driving test seven times."

The marquee went silent, and Dev's new mother-in-law put down her glass.

Aunt Maud rose, gripping her stick. "Seven lives!" she announced to the room. "He saved seven lives! I always knew that boy was a hero."

There was a pause, and then the whole marquee was on its feet, cheering, and Dev's mother-in-law raised her glass to her son-in-law, the lifesaver.

Gordon looked at Dev. Dev looked back, shrugged very slightly, and mouthed: "Leave it." So Gordon raised his own glass. "To Dev," he said, "who has always been there when it mattered."

He sat down to the loudest applause of his life. Later, dancing with Aunt Maud, he found the six laminated cards under her glass of sherry. She had been using them as a coaster, and she winked.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="26553d96-92c3-4ca7-8aa2-d1b918c5d554" title="Gordon Pike" words="0-66, 67-82, 110-118, 119-144, 179-196, 233-246, 295-313, 398-415, 416-425" -->

The best man: a careful, anxious man who rehearses everything because he dreads being found wanting in public. He wants to honour his oldest friend without humiliating himself, and his loyalty runs deeper than his nerve.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="a54dd9e3-ed28-42c5-9bb6-711caff7c939" title="Aunt Maud" words="197-232, 334-358, 426-452" -->

The groom's ninety-two-year-old great-aunt: deaf to most of what is said, utterly sure of herself and quietly mischievous. She hears what she wants to hear, turns a disaster into a triumph, and has had the cue cards all along.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="ca8e9725-d2f7-46a6-b01f-dfc5f7119b39" title="The marquee at Hollinsworth Farm" words="83-109, 119-144, 159-178, 322-333, 359-383" -->

A leaking wedding marquee in a rain-soaked field: a hundred and twenty guests packed under dripping canvas, where every mishap happens in front of everyone.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="5bfe2b1a-64b1-4800-b66e-a4b4aa3f0744" title="Stage fright" words="0-66, 110-118, 119-148, 149-158, 159-196, 233-246, 283-294, 416-425" -->

Gordon's terror of speaking in front of a crowd: the endless rehearsing, the freezing, the noise like a kettle, and how it lifts once he speaks from the heart.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="30c86589-68f2-4eb2-8efb-7ff468cb3b9e" title="Friendship" words="0-17, 233-282, 295-321, 384-415" -->

The forty-one-year friendship of Gordon and Dev: a favour never repaid, a secret kept for years, and forgiveness given with a shrug.

<!-- cell: plot id="d13068ca-a1c7-4b30-b039-620b0c902a5d" title="The lost speech" words="0-17, 67-82, 110-118, 145-148, 149-158, 233-246, 295-321, 322-333, 334-358, 359-383, 416-452" -->

Gordon loses his cue cards, freezes, improvises, blurts out Dev's secret, and is saved when Aunt Maud mishears it as heroism; at the end he finds the cards under her glass.
